<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<templates xml:space="preserve">

  <!-- Numbered steps on core's ARROW statusbar (owner 2026-08-03). ATTRIBUTES ONLY, by design.

       The button keeps its `t-esc="item.label"` exactly as core wrote it; this extension adds a
       hook class and the ordinal, and statusbar_steps.scss draws the digit from
       `content: attr(data-viin-step)`. Three things follow from that, and all three are the reason
       it is written this way:

         1. NO DOM TEXT CHANGES. Core's own Hoot suite asserts statusbar button text exactly
            (`expect(".o_arrow_button_current").toHaveText("first record")`), and so do the sale /
            crm tours. A `<span>` holding the digit would append it to `button.textContent`.

         2. NO LOAD-ORDER DEPENDENCY ON mail. `mail.StatusBarDurationField` is a
            `t-inherit-mode="primary"` COPY of `web.StatusBarField`, and core's template engine
            applies to that copy every extension of the parent whose blockId is lower than the
            copy's own (web/static/src/core/templates.js `_getTemplate`: it walks
            `templateExtensions[parent]` and breaks at `otherBlockId > blockId`). mail's own xpath
            targets `//*[@t-esc='item.label']`, so an extension of ours that REMOVED that t-esc
            would make mail's copy fail to build if we happened to load first. Adding attributes is
            safe in either order, and re-adding the same attribute is idempotent, which is why the
            mail duration copies can be extended separately (viin_brand_mail) without a conflict.

         3. NO GEOMETRY CHANGE. Nothing here touches the clip-path chevron, the container padding
            or the container border. That is the T-2 regression class the D6 stepper caused: core's
            areItemsWrapping() folds the whole bar into one dropdown as soon as the ROOT measures
            taller than its first child, so anything that inflates the measured container breaks the
            widget at every width.

       The marker is decorative: it duplicates position information the surrounding radiogroup
       already conveys (role="radio" + aria-current="step"), so it is generated content and is
       announced by nothing. -->
  <t t-inherit="web.StatusBarField" t-inherit-mode="extension">
    <xpath expr="//button[@role='radio']" position="attributes">
      <attribute name="class" add="o_viin_numbered_step" separator=" "/>
      <attribute name="t-att-data-viin-step">viinStepNumber(item)</attribute>
    </xpath>
  </t>

</templates>
